WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.017
The Mariana Trench is the
deepest point on Earth.

00:00:04.017 --> 00:00:07.640
So deep that if you dropped
Mount Everest inside,

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.315
its peak would still be more than

00:00:09.315 --> 00:00:11.510
a kilometer underwater.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:15.250
So deep that no light reaches the bottom.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.190
And if you could feel
it, the pressure would be

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.990
about equal to the weight of

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:21.540
48 jumbo jets.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.940
Yet, somehow, life finds a way.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.083
Very weird life.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.180
The truth is, at first glance...

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.775
Douglas Bartlett: You
wouldn't be very impressed.

00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:35.724
It's gonna be this

00:00:35.724 --> 00:00:38.624
kind of silky, gray-green sediment.

00:00:38.624 --> 00:00:40.410
Narrator: But if you put out some food,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.840
some of the planet's strangest creatures

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.120
would emerge from the darkness.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.890
Like these.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.670
They're crustaceans called amphipods.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.419
And some individuals can grow

00:00:51.419 --> 00:00:53.178
up to 30 centimeters long,

00:00:53.178 --> 00:00:56.404
or about the size of an American football.

00:00:56.404 --> 00:00:57.821
Now, like lobsters,

00:00:57.821 --> 00:01:00.323
these animals have hard exoskeletons

00:01:00.323 --> 00:01:02.098
made of calcium carbonate,

00:01:02.098 --> 00:01:04.789
which you normally
wouldn't find down here,

00:01:04.789 --> 00:01:07.962
more than 10,000 meters down.

00:01:07.962 --> 00:01:11.710
Because once you pass 4,500 meters or so,

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.351
the pressure is so great

00:01:13.351 --> 00:01:15.501
and the temperature is so low

00:01:15.501 --> 00:01:18.410
that calcium carbonate dissolves.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.010
But these amphipods have a secret weapon.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.240
Or rather, shield.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.748
The crustaceans are covered

00:01:24.748 --> 00:01:26.947
with an armor made of aluminum,

00:01:26.947 --> 00:01:29.160
which they synthesize in their bodies,

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.003
using aluminum found in the seafloor.

00:01:33.003 --> 00:01:34.350
It's a good thing, too,

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.610
because those shells
are their best defense

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.960
against something else
lurking in the abyss.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.318
The Mariana snailfish,

00:01:42.318 --> 00:01:45.630
aka the deepest fish ever discovered,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:46.971
which scientists have seen

00:01:46.971 --> 00:01:50.040
more than 8,000 meters down.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.606
And there are a lot

00:01:51.606 --> 00:01:54.210
of strange things about these fish.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.520
They have flexible bones,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.890
which scientists think helps
them withstand pressure.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.890
They're blind, because,
well, when it's that dark,

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.410
there's no point in seeing.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:06.340
And...

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.660
Bartlett: Their skin is translucent,

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.210
so you can see their internal organs.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.088
Narrator: But they're
also one of the trench's

00:02:13.088 --> 00:02:14.720
top predators.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:15.850
And if you want proof,

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:19.350
check out this terrifying X-ray image.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.620
Now, snailfish are certainly odd.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.010
But then again, you haven't yet met...

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.485
Bartlett: A xenophyophore.

00:02:25.485 --> 00:02:27.168
Narrator: It's a single-celled organism

00:02:27.168 --> 00:02:29.001
akin to a giant amoeba.

00:02:29.001 --> 00:02:31.360
In fact, at about 10 centimeters long,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.918
it's one of the largest

00:02:32.918 --> 00:02:35.784
single-celled organisms on Earth.

00:02:35.784 --> 00:02:38.572
The trench also has what Bartlett calls...

00:02:38.572 --> 00:02:40.130
Bartlett: These little sea pigs.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.670
Narrator: They're small,
transparent sea cucumbers

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.570
that crawl along the ocean floor

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.303
on tentacle-like legs.

00:02:47.303 --> 00:02:50.280
But perhaps the most surprising
thing you'll discover

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.430
inside the Mariana Trench is this:

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.920
Plastic.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.410
In 1998, a remotely operated submersible

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.452
detected a plastic bag

00:03:00.452 --> 00:03:03.720
at 10,898 meters.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.580
And in 2019, an explorer reported

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.720
signs of plastic debris even further down.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.510
What's more, researchers
have found microplastics

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.860
inside the stomachs of amphipods

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.010
in the Challenger Deep.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.707
They called it "the deepest record

00:03:18.707 --> 00:03:21.160
of microplastic ingestion."

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.880
And all that goes to show that even here,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.400
in the most remote place on the planet,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.820
where only four people have ever visited,

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:33.737
there are signs of our
vast impact on the world.

